            <ArticleTitle>Fifteen-minute consultation: A structured approach to the management of hypermobility in a child.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>212-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/archdischild-2013-303882</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present a structured approach for an outpatient consultation of a child with hypermobility.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Review of the literature and a description of the approach commonly adopted in a paediatric out-patient setting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A focused history and examination is the key to reaching an appropriate differential diagnosis and planning management for children with hypermobility.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 10-year-old child is referred to your general paediatric clinic with frequent 'cracking and clicking' of her joints and a history of intermittent aches and pains over the last 6 months.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
